"The engine is loaded and runs, but it has **no data to act on** — so no client list can be frozen or emitted. I'm stopping here rather than fabricating a roster, because inventing client IDs would violate the very privacy contract we bound in the previous step.\n\n**Status**\n\n| Step | Result |\n|---|---|\n| Locate client registry / partition map | ❌ Not found — only the IPython kernel JSON exists |\n| Freeze client-partitioned dataset | ⛔ Blocked (no partitions to hash) |\n| List eligible clients | ⛔ Blocked (no registry + no policy thresholds) |\n\n**One caveat in the code above:** the `opted_in` line returns a scalar `True` when `require_opt_in=False`, and `pd.DataFrame({...})` mixed with a scalar will broadcast — functional, but cleaner to write `pd.Series(True, index=df.index)`. I'll patch that once we wire it to real data.\n\n**To proceed, send me any of these (path or pasted table):**\n\n1. **`client_registry`** — needs `client_id`, `partition_uri`, `row_count`, `n_classes`, `opt_in`, `consent_irb`, `last_seen`.\n2. **Eligibility policy** — your values for `min_rows`, `min_classes`, `require_opt_in`, `max_staleness_days`.\n3. **Round manifest** — `round_id`, `sampling_rate`, `min_clients_per_round`, `seed`.\n\nOnce supplied, `freeze_and_select(...)` will return (a) the frozen partition table hashed for reproducibility, (b) an exclusion breakdown showing *why* each ineligible client was dropped, and (c) the eligible client list — which we then reconcile against the contract's `min_clients_per_round` before signing this round."